  "truthSource": "manual_audit",
  "truthReference": "Independent hand audit of validation/m12/fixtures/c/*.{c,h} against C semantics and the M11 C contract (validated 1.000/1.000 in M11.10; symbols only). Symbols: functions (definitions + prototypes) -> function; structs with bodies -> struct with fields -> property; unions with bodies -> type with fields -> property; enums -> enum with members -> enum_member; typedefs (function pointers, aliases) -> type when the alias differs from the specifier name; object-like macros with bodies -> constant, function-like macros -> macro; file-scope static vars -> variable (private). Noise: include guards (#define NAME with no value body), forward declarations (struct without body — no symbol), function-local variables (no symbol), anonymous typedef'd structs/enums named by their typedef declarator (ONE symbol). Relationships (family contract, matches Java M12.01): contains = REAL container ownership only (struct->field, union->field, enum->member) — never `global contains X`; calls = function -> callee; uses = field access (a->x) and value identifier usage (global var, enum member, local function ref) — never both uses+references for the same pair; references = include dependencies (global -> header) and type usage (declared in-file type identifiers, incl. bare return types); instantiates = allocator calls (malloc/calloc/realloc family — C-specific allocation semantic). Relationship sources are the enclosing FUNCTION name (not block scopes like the legacy `core_init.block_211` dangling identity); top-level file-scope relationships use `global`. Cross-file declarations (e.g. Buffer/Status declared in Module.h, used in Module.c) do not resolve in a per-file parse — production wiring of the companion index is tracked by M12.44 (same note as Java).",
